# Checked local reductions preceding the 14.7.52 classification

For every actual member J of H_z, the F.8 geometry now proves that R₁
is not contained in O₂(J). Four divides each Sylow order in J/O₂(J),
completing the cyclic three-residual exclusion throughout H_z. The remaining
simultaneous-cubic configuration contradicts the original quotient action's
absence of transvections. Thus the five-core, and every prime core above
three, vanishes; F(J/O₂(J)) = O₃(J/O₂(J)), as in 14.7.6. A solvable
J has a noncyclic three-core with an actual normal two-perfect lift marked
by E₉ or the Heisenberg group. Its prime divisors are two and three, and
the original point image now has order three, with full kernel O₂(L₁),
and lies in O₃(J/O₂(J)). The SL₂(3) image in the elementary-nine branch
is excluded by the original quadratic actor; in this branch the literal
two-residual equals the three-core. In the Heisenberg branch the full
Frattini-action kernel is O₃(J/O₂(J)), retaining the inner action.
On a minimal solvable witness, the outside-point cubic lift lies in M,
and every selected normal lift now satisfies O₂(Y) < Y ∩ M. The actual
Heisenberg center has a proper normal cubic lift whose L-commutator
escapes O₂(L). In the elementary-nine branch, the actual point image is
the three-core's fixed cubic line. It and the moved quotient-centralizing
cubic are disjoint and generate the nine-image. A three-core element
outside that image centralizes the point and moves the other line.
The original minimal solvable witness has normal point residual in both
branches, with the exact point two-core kernel retained. T-invariant cyclic
enlargement excludes the elementary-nine branch, including higher three-power
actors. The remaining normal Heisenberg lift escapes M: its nontrivial
derived two-residual would otherwise be killed by the action on L/O₂(L),
contrary to ambient normalizer control. Minimality then identifies this
lift with O²(I), with the full quotient kernel and center marking retained.
Under solvability of the original G₁, this minimal I is proper in G₁.
Its Heisenberg residual embeds normally in the full point quotient, with
centralizer exactly the marked central cubic L₁*. The full-residual
extraspecial exclusion makes that image proper in the full two-residual.
Irreducibility identifies it with the full three-core and excludes a cubic
quotient; the literal remaining quotient is SL₂(3). Its full residual has
order 648 and centralizes the point cubic. A faithful binary six-space
then embeds it in GL₃(4) with index prime to three, forcing three-rank at
least three. Thus the original full point normalizer is nonsolvable.
Source 14.7.7 now supplies the full-star centralizer argument without
full-point solvability, excluding every solvable original local as in
14.7.29. Each local therefore contains an escaping C-component. Its
actual two-core quotient is quasisimple: the Fitting equality above
excludes the nonquasisimple A.3.6 alternatives. The literal reduced local
and its quadratic actor retain the whole component orbit and exact
two-core kernel. Solvable subgroups normalized by L₁T, and in particular
the local solvable radical, lie in M and normalize L₁. The three-core's
full preimage acts on L/O₂(L) with a two-group kernel: an escaping
component and the ambient normalizer bound kill its kernel two-residual.
Consequently the three-core has order at most three. The strict-intersection
argument identifies each nonzero three-core with the literal point image.
Solvable Fitting self-centralization gives the same alternative for the
odd core, completing 14.7.27–28. The component/model classification
through 14.7.52 remains.

This interface re-exports the original point action, seed separation,
two-core action descent, the S₅ quotient in the A₅ residual branch,
and the relative component deduction for both residual alternatives. In
the alternating branch, the original seed excludes the deleted five-point
quotient. A fixed index at most four for an actual residual Sylow constructs
the full equivariant natural quotient with exactly the K-fixed kernel.
The alternating branch supplies a selected critical path in R₁ and the
reciprocal rank comparison. Its involution displacement bound excludes two
noncentral sections. Full commutator generation then gives an irreducible
fixed quotient with the faithful full S₅ action and exact original K-fixed kernel.

The genuine irreducible classification and the full symmetric extension of
the deleted alternative now identify the fixed quotient as natural. The
source-data application discharges uniqueness, the component condition
and natural recognition from the residual alternatives after excluding G₂.
The critical geometry also forces a nontrivial R₁ image and star Sylow
order divisible by four, excluding a cyclic three-group residual at the
original point normalizer. The canonical residual comparison keeps the
original quotient map. In the normal-point branch, the original cubic is
fixed-free on the literal section, R₁* is its Sylow centralizer of index two,
and even displacement forces a nontrivial reciprocal critical actor. An
abelian residual supplies this branch and the plane-centralizer premise of
the original weak-closure implication. The normal-point source reduction now
also proves the plane commutator, QQ_H = R₁, and the selected quadratic
two-offender with the literal reciprocal index. Under the additional rank-one
bound on R₁*, the two reciprocal indices are two, the point commutators are
